Janis Papanagnou wrote:

 > Race success was Dwa:68%, Elf:58%, Orc:51%, Hum:46%, Gno:37%.

The table I posted reveals that your non-humans generally perform
either as good as or considerably better than your humans, which is
what I would expect (like I said, humans are disadvantaged by lack of
infravision, especially when dependent on ranged combat etc).

The exceptions are Gno Arc/Cav/Hea, which explains your "low" success
ratio with gnomes. I wonder why you have problems with these, though,
because your Gno Wiz and Ran perform as expected. Especially Gno Hea
should be one of the easier combinations to ascend, unless one tries
for pacifism.

 > Worst are Tou and Arc with 21% and 23% resp., best are Val and
 > Bar/Wiz with 75% and 68% resp.

For reference, since I try harder, I've ascended 77% Tou, 87% Arc, 75%
Val, 87% Bar and 72% Wiz. All on a rather similiar level IMHO (though
I cherry-pick my role/race combinations and don't play random ones).

 > Please also consider the variance in the role/race data (the
 > difference between 37% and 68%, and between 21% and 75%; it is
 > quite significant).

I already wondered about your underperforming gnomes above #-).

Looking at my own data since January, I don't see such a significant
difference among races. My elves are quite crap, but I've only played
rangers (don't really like them) and two wizards since then. Elves are
still >50%.

Role-wise, my worst are rangers (55%; see comment above) and rogues
(54%), though I know the latter is skewed by my own stupidity (had to
#quit one after exploding a bag of holding right before the ascension
run and another died while meleeing a minotaur instead of zapping the
/ofDeath in main inventory). The rest is >=70%.

So for me, there simply isn't that much difference in regard to role
or race. I still "blame" this largely on proper strategy and playing
style which make role/race negligible.

 > Wishes are rare, very very rare indeed, and the options where
 > you'll get them are very dangerous, so my strategies don't rely
 > on those.

I don't rely on them either, but use any opportunities I get. When my
character is advanced enough, I regularly dip out fountains beginning
from level 1 downwards. While the main reason is to blank scrolls and
potions, uncurse items or make unholy water, I also do it because of
the potential wishes.

As for risks... Thrones are mostly harmless if handled properly and
fountain dipping can be made quite safe with a simple Elbereth cage.
People who quaff from fountains deserve what they get #-).

You've said you keep your first magic lamp for light. I used to do the
same. Now I often take the chance (and 80% isn't that bad!) to wish up
something that gives magic resistance (often an artifact these days),
because MR gives you more options to play with (poly trap dancing to
upgrade your pet(s), or enchanting that shirt/cloak/robe because you
don't have to fear its destruction in a poly trap accident anymore),
along with the obvious advantages.

My last samurai used a (pre-blessed) magic lamp from a Minetown shop
to wish up the Magic Mirror of Merlin. Magic resistance + ESP = win.

 >> wish for Magicbane.
 >
 > Ignoring the faint wish chances; for me that's not an option, since
 > not wishing for artifacts is a standard conduct for me.

Well, conducts make the game harder. If you hamper yourself in this
regard, you also hamper your chances to win. Not the best tactic, if
you actually play to win.

Maybe that's why our numbers above differ so much. I use everything I
can to win while you don't?

 > [*] In my current game, until minetown and sokoban, I found not a
 > single sack or bag or even any chest of box, just an ice box in
 > minetown, then only two cursed ordinary sacks in minetown+1 bones,
 > so I am again travelling at the capacity limits (or rather beyond
 > that limit if we consider the more clever players). And this time
 > it *is* even a human. ;-)

I was a total pack-rat myself #-). I eventually realized that going
around burdened is a really bad habit, especially if you have rather
crappy AC and/or no decent damage output. Now if I have to use a sack
or am even bagless, I make temporary stashes next to the downstairs
and consolidate them regularly, keeping only the most essential items
in inventory or sack. I explore and clean out a few levels, then go
back to my main stash, collecting all the temporary stashes along the
way, often walking back and forth several times. Most items you find
aren't useful "as is" anyway, but require (price) identification, BUC
testing or uncursing, and carrying them around as "dead weight" isn't
sensible while exploring unknown territory.

